\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

\paragraph{Value decomposition.}
Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares calibration MAE across methods. Vanilla 2PL without a contamination parameter (B3, $\gamma = 0$) achieves calibration comparable to the full 2PL+$\gamma$ model: ARC MAE 0.016 vs.\ 0.020, GSM8K 0.006 vs.\ 0.006, MMLU 0.007 vs.\ 0.007. The calibration benefit comes from IRT ability estimation itself, which separates stable capability from item-level noise regardless of whether contamination is explicitly modeled. The IRT model successfully differentiates ability across scales: the mean ability gap between 3B and 1.5B models is $\Delta\theta = 0.50$ (95\% CI $[0.43, 0.57]$) in the pooled analysis, with all eight domain-level estimates also excluding zero (Appendix~\ref{sec:theta_separation}). B3 achieves lower ARC MAE than ContamCal because $\gamma$ at $J = 13$ introduces estimation noise. ContamCal nonetheless provides three capabilities beyond vanilla 2PL: $\gamma$ estimates supply domain-level contamination diagnostics (Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}), the posterior yields credible intervals that propagate signal uncertainty, and gating criteria provide principled exclusion rules for benchmarks where $\gamma$ is non-significant. Although contaminated models exhibit inflated $\theta$, calibration remains accurate because IRT jointly estimates item parameters alongside $\theta_j$, and clean d000 anchors prevent uniform scale inflation.

\paragraph{Oracle ablation confirms decomposition.}
To test whether non-significant $\gamma$ reflects poor signal quality or weak item-level effects, we replace RRF scores with ground-truth binary injection labels (AUC$=$1.0). Even with perfect detection, $\gamma$ remains non-significant: MMLU $\gamma = -0.012$ (CI $[-0.145, 0.119]$, PPP$=$0.41) and ARC $\gamma = +0.118$ (CI $[-0.075, 0.306]$, PPP$=$0.03). The binding constraint is not signal precision but effect heterogeneity: injection inflates some items while disrupting others (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), and a shared $\gamma$ averages over opposing directions. This strengthens the core finding: IRT's value lies in $\theta$ estimation, not $\gamma$-based contamination quantification. Critically, on the full 14,042 MMLU items (${\sim}2\%$ contaminated), vanilla 2PL $\tau$ drops to 0.024 ($p=0.893$) vs.\ $\tau = 0.637$ ($p=0.006$) on the exposure-selected subset, confirming that RRF-based item selection is a necessary preprocessing step for effective calibration.

\paragraph{Gating and negative results.}
ARC passes gating: MAE reduction of 0.014 relative to B0-Raw (0.034 $\to$ 0.020) and $\gamma$ CI excluding zero ($\tau = 0.733$). GSM8K fails gating ($\gamma$ non-significant). Credible interval coverage is benchmark-dependent: MMLU over-covers at all levels (50\%: 83\%, 80\%: 89\%, 95\%: 100\%) while ARC under-covers (50\%: 45\%, 80\%: 65\%, 95\%: 90\%), consistent with its marginal model fit (Limitations). The GSM8K overcorrection ratio is 11$\times$ due to the non-significant $\gamma$ being applied uniformly.

\paragraph{Comparison to baselines.}
B0-Raw shows substantial miscalibration on ARC (MAE$=$0.034) but minimal on MMLU (MAE$=$0.016) where diverse domains partially cancel errors. B1-Remove\&Rescore reduces item count without improving calibration. B4-LinearRegression matches IRT on GSM8K but fails on ARC where nonlinear ability-difficulty interaction matters. B5-Emperor \citep{sun2025emperor} confirms that mitigation approaches do not restore clean performance. B6-DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} produces substantially higher MAE than all other methods (ARC 0.245, GSM8K 0.263; Appendix~\ref{sec:full_baselines}), consistent with overcorrection from fuzzy inference without a measurement-theoretic anchor.

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

\paragraph{Min-K\%++ directional reversal.}
Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items, yielding Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005 and $d = -1.98$ at d100 relative to d000. A practitioner applying Min-K\%++ at this scale would conclude that contaminated items are \textit{less} likely to be memorized. At 1.5B, discrimination turns mildly positive ($d \approx +0.3$); at 3B, moderately positive ($d \approx +0.7$). We hypothesize that SFT injection at 0.5B disrupts the token-level surprisal landscape so severely that contaminated items (overfit at the response level) paradoxically show reduced token-level fluency.

\paragraph{Signal reliability across scales.}
Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} summarizes detection AUC across signal-scale-dosage combinations. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at d005 and above for all scales. S4 (Self-Critique) performs near-random at 0.5B and reaches marginal discrimination at 1.5B+. RRF fusion degrades to S1-only performance because S2 and S4 contribute noise at small scales.

Cross-family analysis with Llama-3.2-3B confirms that RRF exposure scores generalize across architectures, though pooled $\gamma$ loses diagnostic specificity (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_signal_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Detection signal AUC across scale, dosage, and signal type. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $>0.95$ at all scales; S2 (Min-K\%++) reverses at 0.5B; S4 (Self-Critique) is near-random below 1.5B.}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
\label{sec:identifiability}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_identifiability_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{$\gamma$ recovery as a function of signal AUC and ensemble size $J$. Bias $<0.05$ and 95\% CI coverage $\geq90\%$ require $J\geq50$ at AUC$=$0.55, but only $J\geq16$ at AUC$\geq$0.90.}
\label{fig:identifiability_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The simulation study (20 conditions, 10 seeds each, 200 runs) establishes the operating envelope. Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} presents bias and CI coverage as a function of signal quality and ensemble size. Recovery succeeds (bias $< 0.05$, 95\% CI coverage $\geq 90\%$) at AUC as low as 0.55 with $J \geq 50$. Posterior CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$, close to the parametric $J^{-0.5}$ rate. Current IRT-based LLM evaluation operates at $J = 8$--$30$ \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}, well below this threshold. Signal quality is the binding constraint: when AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), even $J = 16$ suffices. Our $J = 13$ results confirm the analysis: calibration via $\theta$ works, but per-domain $\gamma$ remains exploratory.

\subsection{Exploratory Domain Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

